From d06535232d43972290d47c98fd0c571f64fcc77e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: build <build@localhost>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 07:10:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Process the augmentation dots of a chord top down, whatever
 order they were found in

The two dots of a chord whose heads both sit on lines or ledgers (a
third: G4 B4, B4 D5, C4 E4) are stacked one interline apart at the same
abscissa. Such a chord came out undotted at random: the same build on
the same PDF lost its dot in one run and kept it in the next (Killing Me
Softly bar 20, Stumblin' In 17 and 59, Piano Man's accordion 48 and 168,
Songbird 32, Yellow Submarine 64), and on some chords in every run
(Stumblin' In 55, Songbird 27, 43, 48, 78, 79, 127). The rest of the bar
then came half a beat early.

AugmentationDotInter.lookupHeadLink expects the dots top down ("System
dots are already in place or they are processed top down"): a dot
between two heads on lines takes the upper head while it is free, so the
dot below goes to the lower head. DotFactory.lateDotChecks sorted its
candidates by abscissa only, and a stack of dots kept the order in which
SymbolsBuilder had evaluated their glyphs: a glyph cluster is a set from
JGraphT's connectedSets (a HashSet), its seeds sorted by weight only, and
a Glyph's hash includes its run table's orientation, an enum whose
identity hash changes from run to run. Two dots of one weight (every
pair on a page rendered from vectors) came in hash order; on a scan the
heavier came first, every run. Bottom up, the lower dot took the upper
head, the upper dot found no free head within reach, and the chord, one
head of two dotted, was given no dot (countDots: rint(0.5) = 0).

The candidates are now sorted by columns: left to right, a column being
the dots whose abscissa range overlaps that of its leftmost dot, each
column top down. Both orders are total (x, y, width, height), so the
result no longer depends on the order the glyphs were found in. Top down
also suits a lower voice whose dots are printed below its line notes:
the upper dot, between the two heads, takes the upper one.

Between two runs of the build without this, the books' inters differed
in augmentation dots only; with it, three or four runs of each of the
owner's nine songs export byte-identical MusicXML, with identical inters
and dot-head links.

